syntax:
For a class with virtual functions:

// Class declaration
class ClassName {
public:
    virtual void functionName();
    // ... other members
};

// Compiler-generated internal layout:
// Object memory layout:
// [Object Data Members][Pointer to vtable (vptr)]
// vtable:
// static array of function pointers:
// vtable for ClassName: [pointer to ClassName::functionName, ...]

// During runtime, calling a virtual function:
// object->vptr[0]()

// The vptr points to the vtable, which contains pointers to the actual function implementations.

code_example:
class Base {
public:
    virtual void foo() { /* Base implementation */ }
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void foo() override { /* Derived implementation */ }
};

// Object layout in memory:
// [Base subobject][vptr pointing to Base's vtable]
// [Derived subobject][vptr pointing to Derived's vtable]

// Runtime dispatch:
// When calling obj->foo():
// 1. Access vptr in the object memory
// 2. Use vptr to locate vtable
// 3. Retrieve function pointer for 'foo'
// 4. Call the function via the pointer
